    {
      "text": "TRIPLE LINKING NUMBERS, AMBIGUOUS HOPF INV ARIANTS — Three-component links in the 3-dimensional sphere were classiﬁed up to link homotopy by John Milnor in his senior thesis, published in 1954.\n\n**TRIPLE LINKING NUMBERS, AMBIGUOUS HOPF INV ARIANTS**\n\n*AND INTEGRAL FORMULAS FOR THREE-COMPONENT LINKS*\n\n23 pp, 7 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** Three-component links in the 3-dimensional sphere were classiﬁed up to link homotopy by John Milnor in his senior thesis, published in 1954. A complete set of invariants is given by the pairwise linking numbers p,q andr of the components, and by the residue class of one further integer µ, the “triple linking number” of the title, which is well-deﬁned modulo the greatest common divisor of p, q and r. The Borromean rings: p =q =r = 0, µ = ±1 To each such link L we associate a geometrically natural characteristic map gL from the 3-torus to the 2-sphere in such a way that link homotopies of L become homotopies ofgL. Maps of the 3-torus to the 2-sphere were classiﬁed up to homotopy by Lev Pontryagin in 1941.\n\n**Outline.** 1 Statement of results; 2 Background and motivation; Acknowledgements; 3 Explicit formula for the characteristic map ; 3.1 An asymmetric version of the characteristi; 6.1 The fundamental solution of the Laplacian; References\n\n**Extracted claims (proposals, not verified).**\n- Theorem: A. The pairwise linking numbers p, q and r of the link L are\n- Theorem: B. If the pairwise linking numbers p, q and r of the three com-\n- Theorem: s A and B above give a similar formulation of Milnor’s triple linking number\n- Proposition: The fundamental solution of the scalar Laplacian on the 3-torus T 3 is",
